  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dangerous heat expected through early next week.\n\nMid-level high pressure will generally prevail across the region \naiding in above normal temperatures through the period. High \nhumidity will help push heat indices into Heat Advisory range (105-\n109 degrees) for most each day and even into Heat Warning range \n(110+ degrees) for some (mainly across the coastal Counties where \ndewpoints should be greatest). However, it is worth noting that \nforecast confidence regarding heat indices remains lower than \nnormal, especially this weekend, as there is some uncertainty \nregarding temps/dewpoints/rain/cloud coverage. \n\nRemember, heat is the #1 weather-related cause of death so everyone \nis encouraged to take proper precautions. Check out our heat page \n(weather.gov/ilm/heat) for the latest heat forecasts and safety \ninfo. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor coastal flooding expected through the rest of \nthe week, especially along the lower Cape Fear River.\n\nAstronomical influences from the new moon will cause elevated tide \nlevels thru late week. Minor coastal flooding is expected along the \ncoast and along the lower Cape Fear River, especially during the \nevening high tides through today. Along the lower Cape Fear River, \nminor coastal flooding is also possible during the morning high \ntides.",
